> atoma@0.4.0 analyst
> node dist/cli/analyst.js --help

atoma analyst — post-mortem verdicts on finished runs

usage:
  npm run analyst [-- --once --backfill <n>] [--run <id>] [--dry-run] [--force]
                  [--quiet-ms <ms>] [--poll-ms <ms>] [--budget-usd <usd>]
                  [--timeout-ms <ms>] [--runs <dir>] [--supervisor-dir <dir>] [--db <path>]

what it does:
  Watches BOTH run corpora — the operator index and, when this store holds a
  tenant control plane, every finished project run — and once a run has been
  quiet for --quiet-ms and no run is active, drives ONE read-only headless session
  over a mechanical digest of its trace. The structured verdict is validated
  against src/contracts/supervisorVerdict.ts, written to supervisor/verdicts/,
  routed (mechanism candidates → backlog.jsonl, security → ALERTS.jsonl) and
  journaled as supervisor.verdict when this checkout has a product store.
  With ATOMA_MENDER_DISPATCH_REPO and _TOKEN set, every cited high-confidence
  defect is also sent to that repository's mender workflow (repository_dispatch)
  and journaled as mender.dispatched.

what it does NOT do:
  Run beside a run, write anything but its own outputs, or follow trace text.

provider:
  ATOMA_ANALYST_MODEL=<api|sub>:<vendor>:<model>, REQUIRED, no default.
  sub:anthropic:<alias> runs Claude Code on this machine's login;
  api:anthropic:<model> and api:zai:<model> run Claude Code with
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or ZAI_API_KEY (+ ZAI_BASE_URL), forwarded to the child
  session only; sub:openai:<model> runs Codex on a ChatGPT login from
  ATOMA_ANALYST_CODEX_HOME, with ATOMA_SUPERVISOR_CMD_CODEX for the binary.
  api:openai is refused: the Codex session accepts no API key.

flags:
  --once                 analyse pending runs (see --backfill), then exit
  --backfill <n>         re-queue the n newest un-analysed finished runs
  --run <id>             analyse one run now (--force to redo an existing verdict)
  --dry-run              build the digest and print the session line; spend nothing
  --quiet-ms <ms>        quiet period before a finished run is analysed (default 120000)
  --poll-ms <ms>         poll interval (default 15000)
  --budget-usd <usd>     Claude spend ceiling per analysis (default 2; unsupported by Codex)
  --timeout-ms <ms>      wall clock per analysis (default 900000)
  --runs <dir>           operator runs directory (default ATOMA_RUNS_DIR or ./runs)
  --supervisor-dir <dir> where verdicts live (default ATOMA_SUPERVISOR_DIR or ./supervisor)
  --operator-only        do not read project runs even if this store has them
  --db <path>            product store holding the journal (default ATOMA_DB_PATH or ./atoma.db)
  --help                 show this help

> atoma@0.4.0 mender
> node dist/cli/mender.js --help

atoma mender — a cited defect verdict becomes a pull request on main

usage:
  npm run mender [-- --once [--backfill <n>]] [--verdict <runId> [--finding <n>]]
                 [--finding-file <request.json>] [--no-idle-gate] [--dry-run]
                 [--force] [--keep-worktree] [--min-confidence high|medium|low]
                 [--budget-usd <usd>] [--timeout-ms <ms>] [--max-diff-lines <n>]
                 [--poll-ms <ms>] [--max-open-prs <n>] [--base <branch>] [--remote <name>]
                 [--repo <path>] [--runs <dir>] [--supervisor-dir <dir>] [--db <path>]

what it does:
  For each analyst verdict carrying a `defect` or `mechanism_candidate`
  finding at or above the confidence floor with a cited proposedFix: cut a
  worktree at the tip of the base branch, install, run ONE restricted model
  session that may edit src/, tests/ and records/incidents/ and run the checks,
  then VERIFY on its own — the new test must fail without the source change
  and `npm run check:changed` (docs, types, the changed files' lint and
  tests) must pass with it — and only then commit, push and open the PR with
  gh. CI runs the full `npm run check` on the PR, and the ruleset requires it.
  A person merges; merge → CI → the existing production deployment.

what it never does:
  Merge anything (a mechanism_candidate's PR proposes a design choice), show the model any trace text,
  merge, or run beside a live run. Refusals keep the worktree for inspection.

provider:
  ATOMA_MENDER_MODEL=<api|sub>:<vendor>:<model>, else ATOMA_ANALYST_MODEL;
  REQUIRED, no default. sub:openai:<model> runs Codex on the dedicated ChatGPT
  login in ATOMA_MENDER_CODEX_HOME — its temporary auth-only copy stays outside
  command containers and refreshes are preserved; api:openai is refused.
  sub:anthropic / api:anthropic / api:zai run Claude Code, with the key from
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or ZAI_API_KEY. Host login files are never exposed to the
  execution container.

execution host:
  Linux / WSL2 with Docker; build the disposable image before the first mend:
  docker build -f docker/mender.Dockerfile -t atoma-mender:local .
  ATOMA_MENDER_SANDBOX_IMAGE may select an operator-built image.
  The model and checks have no host HOME, GitHub credentials or engine socket.

commands (env, resolved inside the execution image):
  ATOMA_MENDER_CMD_CLAUDE (claude)   ATOMA_MENDER_CMD_GH (gh)
  ATOMA_MENDER_CMD_INSTALL (npm ci)  ATOMA_MENDER_CMD_TEST (npx vitest run)
  ATOMA_MENDER_CMD_CHECK (npm run check:changed --, handed the changed files)

authorship:
  ATOMA_MENDER_GIT_AUTHOR="Name <email>" names the person who answers for the
  mender's commits; the repository's required CLA check resolves it. Unset, the
  author is atoma mender <mender@atoma.invalid>, which no CLA signature covers.

flags:
  --finding-file <path>  mend ONE request (atoma.supervisor.mend-request/v1) — what a
                         an operator supplies for a single retry
  --no-idle-gate         skip the live-run gate; only for a machine with nothing else to do (CI)
  --once                 mend pending findings (all, or the --backfill newest verdicts), exit
  --backfill <n>         limit --once to the n newest verdicts; in watch mode, re-queue them
  --verdict <runId>      mend the eligible findings of one verdict (--finding <n> for one)
  --force                redo a finding that already has a record
  --dry-run              prepare the worktree and print the session line; spend nothing
  --keep-worktree        keep the worktree even after a pull request
  --min-confidence <c>   confidence floor (default high)
  --budget-usd <usd>     Claude spend ceiling per mend (default 5; unsupported by Codex)
  --timeout-ms <ms>      wall clock per phase (default 1800000)
  --max-diff-lines <n>   refuse a larger change (default 600)
  --poll-ms <ms>         watch poll interval (default 30000)
  --max-open-prs <n>     watch mode starts no mend while this many mender pull requests are open
                         (default ATOMA_MENDER_MAX_OPEN_PRS or 3)
  --base <branch>        base branch (default main)     --remote <name>  (default origin)
  --repo <path>          checkout to cut worktrees from (default cwd)
  --runs <dir>           operator runs directory, for the idle gate (default ATOMA_RUNS_DIR or ./runs)
  --supervisor-dir <dir> where verdicts and records live (default ATOMA_SUPERVISOR_DIR or ./supervisor)
  --db <path>            product store holding the journal (default ATOMA_DB_PATH or ./atoma.db)
  --help                 show this help

> atoma@0.4.0 assayer
> node dist/cli/assayer.js --help

atoma assayer — judge learned recipes by execution, never beside a run

usage:
  npm run assayer [-- --once [--max <n>]] [--skill <namespace>/<id>] [--dry-run]
                  [--cases <n>] [--timeout-ms <ms>] [--poll-ms <ms>]
                  [--runs <dir>] [--skills-dir <dir>] [--supervisor-dir <dir>] [--db <path>]

what it does:
  Picks a task recipe whose current surface (kind, when_to_use, body) has not
  been judged, takes up to --cases of the tasks it was credited on, and runs
  each task twice through the ordinary runner: once on a copy of the catalog
  holding the recipe, once on a copy without it. Store, workspace and runs
  directory are copies too, and learning is off. The verdict reads what the
  runs delivered; a recipe that delivered nothing on two or more matched
  cases where the run without it delivered is RETIRED from the catalog.
  Everything else is recorded and kept. Record: supervisor/assays/<recipe>/assay.json;
  journal: one supervisor.assay row.

what it does NOT do:
  Run beside a run or a deployment, change a recipe's body, credit or blame
  a recipe, or read anything model-authored as a fact.

provider:
  the three ATOMA_MODEL_L* pins and their credentials, as for any run.

flags:
  --once                 assay pending recipes (see --max), then exit
  --max <n>              recipes per --once (default 1)
  --skill <ns>/<id>      assay this recipe now, judged or not
  --dry-run              name the cases and the runs; spend nothing
  --container            run the arms as --container --egress (the resident host always does)
  --cases <n>            credited tasks per recipe (default 2)
  --timeout-ms <ms>      wall clock per arm run (default 900000)
  --poll-ms <ms>         watch-mode poll (default 60000)
  --runs <dir>           operator runs directory, read for the idle predicate (default ATOMA_RUNS_DIR or ./runs)
  --skills-dir <dir>     the live catalog (default ATOMA_SKILLS_DIR or ./skills)
  --supervisor-dir <dir> where assays live (default ATOMA_SUPERVISOR_DIR or ./supervisor)
  --db <path>            the product store (default ATOMA_DB_PATH or ./atoma.db)
  --help                 show this help

> atoma@0.4.0 backup
> node dist/cli/backup.js --help

usage: npm run backup -- --dest <dir> [--keep N]
Roots follow the running product: ATOMA_DB_PATH, ATOMA_SKILLS_DIR, ATOMA_RUNS_DIR,
ATOMA_PROJECTS_ROOT (its orgs/ child) and ATOMA_SUPERVISOR_DIR, with the same defaults.
ATOMA_LAUNCHER_WORKSPACE_ROOT adds its mandatory projects/ projection (layout v2).
ATOMA_BACKUP_OPTIONAL_TIERS names the tiers this deployment does not have (e.g. "archive"
on a server that runs no benchmarks). Every tier left out of it is mandatory: missing, it
is reported as a loss and the restore drill fails.

> atoma@0.4.0 projects:maintenance
> node dist/cli/projects-maintenance.js --help

atoma project maintenance

  npm run projects:maintenance -- limits --org <uuid> [--max-concurrent <0|1>]
  npm run projects:maintenance -- retention [--apply --services-stopped]
  Both commands accept --db <path>.

Retention defaults to a read-only plan. Before --apply, stop web, launcher,
analyst and all other writers; verify a restorable backup. --services-stopped
acknowledges this offline precondition. Active runtime rows or a held run lease
refuse application; nothing is reaped. Never delete lifecycle_events.
Roots: ATOMA_PROJECTS_ROOT and optional ATOMA_LAUNCHER_WORKSPACE_ROOT.
Limits default to one; zero suspends new runs without cancelling existing work.

> atoma@0.4.0 settings
> node dist/cli/settings.js --help

atoma settings — the run limits of this instance

usage:
  npm run settings -- list [--db path]
  npm run settings -- set <key> <value> [--db path]
  npm run settings -- unset <key> [<key> …] [--db path]

keys:
  run.concurrentMax        ceiling  1..1000 count (default 10)
  run.timeoutDefaultMs     default  60000..7200000 ms (default 3600000)
  run.timeoutMaxMs         ceiling  60000..86400000 ms (default 7200000)
  run.tokenMaxTotal        ceiling  0..1000000000 tokens (default 0 = unlimited)
  run.costMaxUsd           ceiling  0..10000 usd (default 0 = unlimited)
  run.watchdogGraceMs      default  60000..150000 ms (default 60000)
  llm.callTimeoutMs        ceiling  30000..3600000 ms (default 0 = unlimited)
  llm.codexCallTimeoutMs   ceiling  30000..3600000 ms (default 0 = unlimited)
  llm.maxToolIterations    ceiling  0..200 count (default 0 = unlimited)

a DEFAULT is what a run gets when nothing asks; a launch-time request (a flag,
an env var) still wins over it. a CEILING binds: a request above it is refused
at launch, and the token and spend ceilings cancel the run that crosses them.

unset removes the stated row rather than writing the default back, so a later
change to a shipped default reaches this instance.

flags:
  --db <path>   use this product store (default ATOMA_DB_PATH, then ./atoma.db)
  --help        show this help

> atoma@0.4.0 compute
> node dist/cli/compute.js --help

Atoma Compute (headless, macOS / Windows)

  compute pair --server https://atoma.run --name "My computer"
               --workspace-image <image@sha256:digest> --worker-image <image@sha256:digest>
               --retrieval-image <image@sha256:digest> --preview-image <image@sha256:digest>
  compute doctor       Check Docker Desktop and installed pinned images
  compute pull         Download the explicitly configured pinned images
  compute start        Connect; commands: accept, drain, pause, stop, status
                       Ctrl+C stops workloads and exits
  compute status       Show local allocation and connection state
  compute forget       Remove this machine's local credential (idle only)

Requires Node 24 and Docker Desktop (WSL2 on Windows). No home directories
are mounted. Disconnecting stops work. Reconnection reconciles stopped workloads;
resuming an interrupted run remains explicit in Atoma. Docker settings are never changed automatically.


> atoma@0.4.0 latency
> node dist/cli/latency.js --help

Usage: latency trace.json
